# Chapter 8: where a setting comes from, a broken link, Terraform drift and the repair
#
# Service A reads `${TABLE_NAME}` in application.properties. Where does the value come from? The ECS task definition maps it
# to an SSM parameter, declared in Terraform, which exists (or not) in the environment. `config.chain` follows every
# placeholder through those links, offline, over a snapshot of the environment's parameters that `config.refresh` fetches.
# This chapter deletes a parameter behind Terraform's back, sees the broken link and the drift, and repairs it with Terraform.
# In the IDE: open estate/services/service-a/src/main/resources/application.properties and Ctrl+click `${TABLE_NAME}`: the
# template line, the Terraform declaration and the parameter. After step 5 a refreshed snapshot underlines it as missing.
# @env local

### shell paths
# Terraform operations take the root directory; ask the shell where this checkout is, and the workspace's name.
# @cwd ..
# @capture root = out
pwd | tr -d '\n'

### shell workspace
# @capture ws = out
printf %s "$DLB_WS"

### op config.refresh snapshot
# Parameters (never decrypted) and secret metadata (never values) of `local`, into config/local.edn of the workspace.
# @assert $.parameters > 30
{"env": "local"}

### op config.chain chain
# Every placeholder of A with its task definition entry, the template line, the Terraform line and the parameter.
# @assert $.entries[0].placeholder == TABLE_NAME
# @assert $.entries[0].taskdef.line == 14
# @assert $.entries[0].parameter.status == present
# @assert $.broken == []
{"name": "a"}

### aws ssm.DeleteParameter delete
# Any AWS operation is a step (`### aws <service>.<Operation>`, chapter 9): here the break, done behind Terraform's back.
{"Name": "/estate/common/table-name"}

### op config.refresh snapshot-again
{"env": "local"}

### op config.chain broken
# The chain names the missing parameter; `--json` of `dlb config chain` gives the template and Terraform lines of the link.
# @assert $.broken[0].kind == parameter-missing
# @assert $.broken[0].reason contains /estate/common/table-name
{"name": "a"}

### op terraform.drift drift
# Terraform's declared resources (`terraform show -json`, reads only) against what exists in the environment.
# In the IDE: tool window → AWS → Terraform drift… marks the missing parameter in the tree.
# @assert $.missing[0].name == /estate/common/table-name
{"dir": "{{root}}/estate/terraform/floci"}

### op terraform.local plan
# `terraform.local` plans or applies a root onto a LOCAL environment only (a remote one is refused). The estate's root needs
# this workspace's container names as variables (the IDE's Plan/Apply buttons cannot pass them yet: BUGS.md B2).
# @assert $.summary contains Plan:
{"dir": "{{root}}/estate/terraform/floci",
 "vars": {"container_endpoint": "http://deliberator-{{ws}}-floci:4566",
          "service_a_url": "http://deliberator-{{ws}}-service-a:8081",
          "db_url": "jdbc:postgresql://deliberator-{{ws}}-postgres:5432/deliberator"}}

### op terraform.local apply
# @timeout 5m
# @assert $.summary contains Apply complete
{"dir": "{{root}}/estate/terraform/floci", "action": "apply",
 "vars": {"container_endpoint": "http://deliberator-{{ws}}-floci:4566",
          "service_a_url": "http://deliberator-{{ws}}-service-a:8081",
          "db_url": "jdbc:postgresql://deliberator-{{ws}}-postgres:5432/deliberator"}}

### op terraform.drift no-drift
# @assert $.missing == []
{"dir": "{{root}}/estate/terraform/floci"}

### op config.refresh snapshot-repaired
{"env": "local"}

### op config.chain repaired
# @assert $.broken == []
{"name": "a"}
